Good. `disconnect()` doesn't persist. The invites manager (`_persist` at 1531) is a separate class with its own `configFile` — but is the invites manager created per-dial in `_tryDial`? No — `_tryDial` creates a `HubClient` directly; the invites class looks separate (HubInvites or similar). So after disconnect, the dial client has no pending persists... except: `_persistEnrolled` is unawaited. Could it still be in flight when `_tryDial` returns ok? The poll captures only AFTER the secret is on disk — i.e., after `target.writeAsString` completed (the last await in persistDapConfig). `_persistEnrolled` then calls `onNotice?.call('enrolled: client secret persisted')` — done. So by capture time the unawaited persist has completed its write. There is one subtlety: persistDapConfig's write is `await target.writeAsString(...)` — writeAsString resolves when the write issued/completed (IOSink write is buffered...). Actually `File.writeAsString` completes when the write is done (it adds the string to the sink and waits for close). So yes, by the time the poll can read the secret from disk via `readAsStringSync`, the content is on disk. So post-disconnect there are no other writers. The claim "CLI is then the only writer, so it is deterministic" holds for the enroll path.

For the cached-dial path (enroll=false): no poll, no capture. `_issuedSecret` stays null (unless an earlier enroll attempt in the same controller set it). Fast path: secret on disk → true. If dropped → repair impossible → exit 1 loudly. Safe.

